  "summary": "An American style featuring extensive use of shingles and irregular forms.",
  "description": "Characterized by seamless wall surfaces covered in shingles, asymmetrical massing, and complex rooflines. It emerged as a reaction against the excessive ornamentation of other Victorian styles, emphasizing natural materials and integration with the landscape.",
  "culturalContext": "The Shingle Style (1874-1900) — invented by American architects (McKim, Mead & White's Shingle-style summerhouses, William Ralph Emerson) for Newport and the New England coast — wrapped asymmetrical massing (turrets, deep porches, gambrels) in one continuous cedar-shingle skin, walls and roofs alike, producing informal, ruggedly elegant resort houses. It democratized the free planning of Queen Anne into American vernacular luxury and seeded the modern \"beach house\" ideal; Vincent Scully's 1955 study canonized it.",
